  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Mortevivum has already established measurable scholarly uptake, with 29 OpenAlex citations placing it in the top 1% most-cited works for its field and year. Its citation profile shows especially strong resonance in photography, race, history, and visual culture, alongside evidence of relevance to Quality Education.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Kimberly Juanita Brown\u2019s Mortevivum, published by The MIT Press in 2024, is drawing sustained academic attention for its examination of photography\u2019s relationship to antiblackness, colonial history, and visual representation. The book\u2019s citation record indicates clear uptake across adjacent humanities and social-science fields, with citations clustering in book chapters and paratextual material as well as journal articles, suggesting that it is being used in broader scholarly argumentation rather than only as a standalone reference.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape is notably interdisciplinary. References to the book appear in work concerned with photography and visual culture, race and American history, cultural identity, and colonialism, which aligns closely with the book\u2019s own conceptual framing around genocide, colonialism, history, gender studies, and photography. This pattern suggests that Mortevivum is functioning as a significant interpretive resource for scholars working at the intersection of visual analysis and racial history.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also shows early indicators of broader reach beyond conventional citation counts. The book is held in WorldCat, appears in OpenAIRE, and has a small amount of web attention, including a Substack mention. In reader-reception metadata, it is listed as a finalist for the ACLS Open Access Book Prize and Arcadia Open Access Publishing Award in the multimodal category, which points to recognition for both scholarly and format-related contribution.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the available evidence portrays Mortevivum as an influential recent scholarly book with strong academic traction and clear thematic fit with education-oriented dissemination. The record is still developing in areas such as public events and wider discourse, but the citation base already shows that the book is being taken up actively within humanities scholarship, especially where photography, visual culture, and histories of race and colonialism intersect.</p>",
